    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Recurrent sudden episodes of unresponsiveness for two years, two or three a month: he &apos;blanks out&apos;, stops speaking, slumps or sinks to the floor with his eyes closed for two to ten minutes, and afterwards is tearful and tired, with no tongue biting, incontinence, or injury; first during a period of overwork and little sleep at college; with low mood and anxiety; unchanged on levetiracetam; under evaluation for epilepsy, functional (dissociative) seizures, syncope, and other causes</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R40.4</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem echoing his documented overwork, infirm health, and low spirits, and the later accounts of sudden attacks; superseded by the diagnosis at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Taking an antiseizure medicine (levetiracetam) started without a confirmed diagnosis of epilepsy</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z79.899</td><td>S — Synthetic medication problem for teaching about avoiding unneeded antiseizure medicines; resolved when the medicine was stopped.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Functional/dissociative seizures (psychogenic nonepileptic seizures), documented by video-EEG: two habitual events captured with eyes closed and resisting opening, fluctuating unresponsiveness, and a normal waking EEG throughout, with no ictal or postictal change; normal interictal EEG and brain MRI; epilepsy and syncope excluded</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F44.5</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis. His infirm health, overwork, and low spirits in youth are documented (D); retrospective explanations of his later attacks, including epilepsy, are disputed (R). This record does not assert what his attacks were.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Generalized anxiety disorder: worry about his future, his health, and his studies most days for more than a year, with restlessness, poor sleep, and muscle tension (GAD-7 14 at intake)</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F41.1</td><td>S — Synthetic diagnosis made at the neuropsychiatric assessment during video-EEG monitoring.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate: low mood, loss of interest, poor sleep and concentration, and a sense that he will not live long, for about eighteen months (PHQ-9 13 at intake); no suicidal ideation or intent</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F32.1</td><td>S — Synthetic diagnosis echoing, without asserting, his documented letter expecting an early death.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

