<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Functional/dissociative seizures, documented by video-EEG (F44.5)</content>. Both habitual events were recorded: eyes closed and resisting opening, fluctuating unresponsiveness for minutes, and slow side-to-side head movements, with a continuous normal waking EEG and no ictal or postictal change. With a typical history and normal interictal EEG and MRI, this meets the ILAE &apos;documented&apos; level of certainty for functional/dissociative seizures. Epilepsy is excluded as the cause of these events: a focal impaired-awareness seizure would be expected to change the EEG, the eyes are usually open, and the events have not responded to levetiracetam. Syncope is excluded by the duration, the closed eyes, the normal ECG and orthostatic vital signs, and the normal EEG during unresponsiveness. Dr. Okafor-Haverford agrees and has explained the diagnosis to him. [S]<list><item>Diagnosis explained again today in plain words and in writing: a real and common condition in which the brain switches off awareness under strain; not epilepsy and not put on; treatable.</item><item>Taper levetiracetam over three weeks and stop it.</item><item>CBT-informed psychotherapy, twelve weekly sessions, starting in two weeks.</item><item>Keep the event diary and use the first-aid card.</item><item>Do not drive yet, including during the taper.</item><item>Review in six weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Recurrent sudden episodes of unresponsiveness (R40.4)</content>. Explained by functional/dissociative seizures; superseded by that diagnosis. [S]<list><item>Managed under the functional seizure problem.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Taking an antiseizure medicine without a confirmed diagnosis (Z79.899)</content>. Levetiracetam has not changed his events and has made him irritable; with epilepsy excluded it should be withdrawn. [S]<list><item>Levetiracetam 250 mg twice daily for two weeks, 250 mg at night for one week, then stop.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Generalized anxiety disorder (F41.1)</content>. GAD-7 13; worry about his future, health, and studies with restlessness and poor sleep. [S]<list><item>Addressed within the psychotherapy; reassess with GAD-7 at each visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, moderate (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 12, item 9 scored 0; no history of mania or hypomania on interview. Psychotherapy first; an SSRI will be offered if symptoms persist. [S]<list><item>Safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and clinic number confirmed.</item><item>PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at every visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>12, down from 13; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7</td><td>With the PHQ-9 when anxiety accompanies depression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>13, down from 14</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior; rifle locked with ammunition separate; protective factors unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Bipolar screen at the initial depression workup</td><td>Once, at the initial workup</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No past manic or hypomanic episode on interview; no Mood Disorder Questionnaire score recorded</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Video-EEG monitoring to capture habitual events</td><td>Once, to establish the diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two habitual events with a normal EEG throughout; normal interictal EEG; ILAE &apos;documented&apos; level</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain MRI with an epilepsy protocol</td><td>Once, in the diagnostic workup</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal, last week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Event diary: frequency, duration, triggers, warning sensations, and injuries</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two or three events a month before admission; two recorded on the unit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine review</td><td>At diagnosis and until withdrawn</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Levetiracetam without benefit and with irritability; taper over three weeks begins today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No antidepressant started; psychotherapy first</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurology consultation (epilepsy and functional neurological disorder)</td><td>For recurrent events of uncertain cause</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen on the unit; diagnosis made and explained</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>CBT-informed psychotherapy for functional seizures</td><td>Once the diagnosis is accepted</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Twelve weekly sessions starting in two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving</td><td>Every visit until cleared</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not driving; not during the taper; may resume after at least three months free of events, with the neurologist&apos;s agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep, studies, and first-aid plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fixed bedtime with at least seven hours; first-aid card for friends and his tutoring workplace</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant mood-stabilizer, or antipsychotic monitoring</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not taking any; no bipolar features</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Cause of the episodes established by the second visit. Outcome by 2026-04-30: Functional/dissociative seizures documented by video-EEG; epilepsy and syncope excluded [S]</item><item>Antiseizure medicine stopped safely. In progress; target 2026-06-11 [S]</item><item>Events reduced by at least half, then free of events for three months. In progress; target 2026-12-31 [S]</item><item>Depression and anxiety improved by at least 50 percent (PHQ-9 and GAD-7). In progress; target 2026-12-31 [S]</item><item>No suicidal thoughts. In progress; target 2026-12-31 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); LaFrance WC Jr, et al. Minimum requirements for the diagnosis of psychogenic nonepileptic seizures: a staged approach: a report from the International League Against Epilepsy Nonepileptic Seizures Task Force. Epilepsia 2013;54(11):2005-2018 (PMID 24111933) (2013); Hingray C, et al. Functional/dissociative seizures: Proposal for a new diagnostic label and definition by the ILAE task force. Epilepsia 2025;66(11):4162-4182 (PMID 40884444) (2025); Fisher RS, et al. Operational classification of seizure types by the International League Against Epilepsy. Epilepsia 2017;58(4):522-530 (PMID 28276060) (2017); Hall-Patch L, et al. Acceptability and effectiveness of a strategy for the communication of the diagnosis of psychogenic nonepileptic seizures. Epilepsia 2010;51(1):70-78 (PMID 19453708) (2010); Stone J, et al. Explanation as treatment for functional neurologic disorders. Handb Clin Neurol 2016;139:543-553 (PMID 27719870) (2016); LaFrance WC Jr, et al. Multicenter pilot treatment trial for psychogenic nonepileptic seizures: a randomized clinical trial. JAMA Psychiatry 2014;71(9):997-1005 (PMID 24989152) (2014); Goldstein LH, et al. Cognitive behavioural therapy for adults with dissociative seizures (CODES): a pragmatic, multicentre, randomised controlled trial. Lancet Psychiatry 2020;7(6):491-505 (PMID 32445688) (2020); Tolchin B, et al. Seizures, Driver Licensure, and Medical Reporting Update: An AAN Position Statement. Neurology 2025;104(7):e213459 (PMID 40073306) (2025); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week review; psychotherapy starts in two weeks; call sooner for events that differ from his usual ones, injury, or any thoughts of self-harm. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-11: six-week review after the diagnosis: levetiracetam withdrawn, psychotherapy under way, mood and anxiety. [S]</paragraph></text>

